    "body": "### Problem\n\nRun the following command.\n\n```bash\nnvim --clean +'tnoremap <F6> <cmd>wincmd w<cr>' +'new' +'term /tmp/c.sh' +'startinsert'\n```\n\nIt opens a normal window and a terminal window running the script. Press F6 from the terminal window goes to the next window. It remains in terminal mode if the next window is a terminal.\n\n`/tmp/c.sh` contains:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\ni=0\nfor _ in {1..100}; do\n  printf '%d\\n' \"$((i++))\"\ndone\nprintf '\\033[3A'\nwhile true; do\n  printf '\\033[3B'\n  printf '%d\\n' \"$((i++))\"\n  printf '\\033[3A'\n  sleep 0.5\ndone\n```\n\nIt just print all numbers sequentially, except that it backs up 3 lines after each numbers being printed, so the cursor does not stay at the bottom of the terminal.\n\n(Motivation: coding agents in terminal. Evidently the cursor location is not at the very bottom of the screen, since it's above the status bar.)\n\nCurrently, the terminal will stop scrolling down the moment it is left (press `<F6>` in the example above).\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nIt should keep scrolling down (if the user were in terminal mode instead of normal mode when they left the terminal, which is the case in the situation above).\n\nMy understanding is neovim makes the terminal scrolls down if and only if the cursor is at the last line. So this workaround suffices\n\n```vim\nauto WinLeave * if &buftype == \"terminal\" && mode()==#'t' | call cursor(line('$'),1) | endif\n```\n\n(Unfortunately the workaround above doesn't work when the user use the mouse to click on another window.)\n\n<details><summary>Workaround that handles mouse click</summary>\n\n```vim\nfunction s:OnLeftMouseExpr()\n\tif getmousepos().winid != win_getid()\n\t\tlet g:last_mouse_time = reltime()\n\tendif\n\treturn \"\\<LeftMouse>\"\nendfunction\n\ntnoremap <expr> <LeftMouse> <sid>OnLeftMouseExpr()\n\n\" ... later ...\n\n\t\tif mode()==#'t' || (exists('g:last_mouse_time') && reltimefloat(reltime(g:last_mouse_time)) < 0.05)\n\t\t\tcall cursor(line('$'),1)\n```\n\n</details>\n\nSeems better if it works out of the box though. After all, \"cursor position\" isn't too meaningful in terminal mode, since once the user goes to terminal-normal, vim's cursor position is reset to the terminal's cursor position. (Or is it used for something else that I don't know of?)",
